timestamp=2026-11-04T09:52:07.355Z level=INFO run=714ca7dd message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import pearsonr\n\nstations = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/stations.csv')\nreadings = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/readings.csv')\nw_readings = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/weather_readings.csv')\n\nreadings['timestamp'] = pd.to_datetime(readings['timestamp'])\nw_readings['timestamp'] = pd.to_datetime(w_readings['timestamp'])\n\npm25 = readings[readings['pollutant']=='PM25'].pivot(\n    index='timestamp', columns='station_id', values='current_reading'\n)\npm10 = readings[readings['pollutant']=='PM10'].pivot(\n    index='timestamp', columns='station_id', values='current_reading'\n)\nw = w_readings.groupby('timestamp')[['temperature_c', 'humidity_pct', 'wind_speed_m_s', 'rain_accumulation_mm']].mean()\n\n# Now let us map all three clues to candidates\n# Clue 1: \"One problematic station broadly follows local patterns, but not with the same rhythm as its peers.\"\n# -> Station where max correlation to peer median is achieved at significant non-zero lag, and correlation improves a lot with lag\n# ST058 (PM25): lag +4 gives corr 0.447 (vs ~0.279 at 0). That’s a big improvement and lag is substantial.\n# Also ST014 lag -3: corr improves from 0.323 to 0.454. Also significant.\n# ST070 (PM10): lag -3, corr 0.431 vs 0.272. Also possible.\n\n# Clue 2: \"Another looks plausible point-by-point yet its swings are unusually muted compared with comparable local stations.\"\n# -> Good correlation at lag 0, but low cv_ratio (variance damped). ST051 (PM25): corr 0.708, cv_ratio 0.765 - very muted but tracks well. ST040 (PM10): also muted (cv_ratio 0.774) but corr 0.385 only; also has other issues (resid correlated with wind). But “looks plausible point-by-point yet swings unusually muted” - high corr + muted swings fits ST051 best. Also ST045 (PM10) muted but corr higher? Let us check ST051’s shape vs peers.\n\n# Clue 3: \"A third appears ordinary in aggregate, but its remaining deviations line up a bit too neatly with one environmental driver.\"\n# -> Ordinary aggregate stats (corr reasonable at lag 0, cv not extreme), but after removing common trend, residuals strongly correlate with some weather variable.\n# ST039 (PM25): corr to median ~0.758, cv_ratio ~1.69 (swings not muted), but resid vs wind_speed_m_s = -0.628. That’s strong coupling of deviations to wind. Also ST040 (PM10): resid strongly coupled to wind (-0.673), but also muted swings and corr only 0.385 - so maybe it fits clue 2? Or clue 3? Wait look at the exact wording.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
