timestamp=2026-11-04T09:54:01.130Z level=INFO run=714ca7dd message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import pearsonr\n\nstations = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/stations.csv')\nreadings = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/readings.csv')\nw_readings = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/weather_readings.csv')\n\nreadings['timestamp'] = pd.to_datetime(readings['timestamp'])\nw_readings['timestamp'] = pd.to_datetime(w_readings['timestamp'])\n\npm25 = readings[readings['pollutant']=='PM25'].pivot(\n    index='timestamp', columns='station_id', values='current_reading'\n)\npm10 = readings[readings['pollutant']=='PM10'].pivot(\n    index='timestamp', columns='station_id', values='current_reading'\n)\nw = w_readings.groupby('timestamp')[['temperature_c', 'humidity_pct', 'wind_speed_m_s', 'rain_accumulation_mm']].mean()\n\n# Now map to the three clues explicitly\n\n# Clue 1: \"One problematic station broadly follows local patterns, but not with the same rhythm as its peers.\"\n# -> This suggests correlation improves significantly when we account for lag / timing offset. Also \"not with the same rhythm\".\n# ST058: corr0 is low (0.279), but best_corr jumps to 0.447 at lag +4 (16 hours). That means it follows the regional pattern but is phase-shifted. That fits \"not with the same rhythm\" perfectly.\n\n# Clue 2: \"Another looks plausible point-by-point yet its swings are unusually muted compared with comparable local stations.\"\n# -> Point-by-point plausibility suggests decent correlation at lag 0. \"Swings unusually muted\" means low cv_ratio relative to peers.\n# ST051: corr0 is high (0.708) at lag 0, cv_ratio is low (0.765, muted). Also no big lag improvement. That fits clue 2.\n\n# Clue 3: \"A third appears ordinary in aggregate, but its remaining deviations line up a bit too neatly with one environmental driver.\"\n# -> \"Ordinary in aggregate\" - corr0 decent, cv_ratio not obviously extreme? ST039: corr0=0.758 (good), cv_ratio=1.759 (swings not muted - actually amplified). But its residuals (deviations after removing common regional trend) correlate strongly with wind_speed_m_s: |resid vs wind| = 0.628. That means after accounting for regional pattern, what's left over is tightly coupled to wind. \"Deviations line up a bit too neatly with one environmental driver\" - fits ST039.\n# Also ST040 (PM10): has strong resid-wind correlation (-0.673) AND also muted swings (cv_ratio 0.774) AND lag issues (best_corr at lag +1 improves a lot). ST040 seems problematic in multiple ways. But the instructions say \"Avoid selecting multiple stations that exhibit the same pattern if other distinct anomalies are present.\" And \"The faulty stations exhibit different types of anomalous behavior.\" So we need 3 different stations, each matching a different clue.\n\n# Map carefully:\n# Clue 1 -> rhythm/lag issue: ST058 (PM25) is the clearest case.\n# Clue 2 -> muted swings but plausible point-by-point: ST051 (PM25). Also check ST040 has muted swings but its correlation is poor at lag0 and also has strong weather coupling; that’s mixed. ST045 also muted but let us look up - ST045 had corr0 0.516, cv_ratio 0.777, no strong resid-weather. But is it \"plausible point-by-point\"? Corr 0.516 is moderate. ST051 has corr 0.708 - stronger case for \"plausible point-by-point\".\n# Clue 3 -> deviations align too neatly with environmental driver after removing common trend: ST039 (PM25) fits this - good aggregate correlation, but strong resid correlation with wind. That’s distinct from muted swings.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
