timestamp=2026-11-04T09:54:50.008Z level=INFO run=714ca7dd message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Let us summarize the best mapping\n# Clue 1 (rhythm/lag): \"One problematic station broadly follows local patterns, but not with the same rhythm as its peers.\"\n# Candidates with lag shift: ST058 (PM25, lag+4, corr0 low but improves), ST014 (PM25, lag-3), ST070 (PM10, lag-3), ST040 (PM10, lag+1) also has shift but mixed issues.\n# ST058 fits cleanly - follows patterns but shifted rhythm. Also the prompt says \"One problematic station...\" - distinct from others.\n\n# Clue 2 (muted swings, plausible point-by-point): ST051 (PM25) - corr0 high (0.708), cv_ratio < 1 (0.765), muted. Very clean match.\n\n# Clue 3 (deviations align too neatly with one environmental driver): ST039 (PM25) - corr0 good (0.758), no lag issue, but resid vs wind_speed_m_s = -0.628. That’s the key signal. Also appears \"ordinary in aggregate\" as stated.\n\n# What about ST040? It has strong resid-wind correlation too (-0.673). But it also has muted swings (cv_ratio 0.774) and poor corr0 (0.385) - multiple issues. Also lag shift. That makes it look like it has multiple problems - but we need stations with *different* types of anomalous behavior, and avoid selecting multiple stations with same pattern if others exist. ST040’s dominant weirdness is mixed; easier to pick cleaner representatives.\n\n# Another check: ST045 (PM10) is muted (cv_ratio 0.777) like clue 2 but for PM10. But ST051 is PM25 and fits perfectly. Is there another PM10 station that fits clue 2 better? Or maybe the faulty stations are a mix across pollutants? The task just says \"station_id\" values.\n\n# Also ST060 (PM10): has lag issues and also many missing values. But missingness itself shouldn’t be the main signal per prompt.\n\n# Best clean set:\n# - Clue 1 (rhythm/lag): ST058\n# - Clue 2 (muted swings, plausible): ST051  \n# - Clue 3 (deviations too neatly aligned with env driver): ST039\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
